WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:09.560
Secure session implementation starts with the basics: use TLS consistently, rely on mature framework session features, and validate session state on the server side.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:16.740
TLS helps protect identifiers in transit, but it does not replace cookie attributes, lifecycle controls, or authorization.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.240
Framework defaults are often strong, but they still need review.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:29.580
Developers should understand how the framework generates identifiers, stores state, sets cookie attributes, handles rotation, and invalidates sessions.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.040
CSRF protections matter when browsers automatically send session cookies.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:41.700
SameSite can help, but applications may still need CSRF tokens or framework protections depending on the workflow and compatibility requirements.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.400
Least privilege also applies to sessions.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:51.100
The session should carry only the context needed for decisions, and authorization should still be checked for each protected resource.

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:55.000
A valid session does not mean the user can perform every action.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.820
Monitoring and audit logging make session controls operational.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:12.960
Log security-relevant events such as login, logout, session renewal, password change, MFA change, privilege change, suspicious session use, and administrative session activity without logging the session secret itself.

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:21.980
Device awareness can support risk decisions at a high level, such as recognizing new devices or unusual patterns, but it should be used carefully and transparently.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:29.000
Avoid custom session systems unless there is a strong reason and enough expertise to design, test, and maintain them safely.
